# Structural non-identifiability fixture (#446/#468).
# The single species S() decays through TWO parallel first-order channels, k1 and k2,
# so the observable Stot follows S(t) = S0 * exp(-(k1 + k2) * t): only the SUM k1 + k2
# is observable, never k1 or k2 individually. Fixing either rate and re-optimizing the
# other (the profile-likelihood re-optimization) trivially compensates along the
# k1 + k2 = const manifold, so each rate profiles FLAT -- the textbook signature of a
# structurally non-identifiable parameter -- while S0 (the intercept) stays identifiable.
# Because k1 + k2 = 0.3, the zero-noise trajectory is identical to e2e_ode_decay.bngl's
# (S0 = 100, k = 0.3), so the same analytic decay .exp is the oracle.
begin model

begin parameters
  S0  100
  k1  0.15
  k2  0.15
end parameters

begin molecule types
  S()
end molecule types

begin seed species
  S()  S0
end seed species

begin observables
  Molecules Stot S()
end observables

begin reaction rules
  S() -> 0  k1
  S() -> 0  k2
end reaction rules

end model

begin actions
  generate_network({overwrite=>1})
  simulate({method=>"ode",t_start=>0,t_end=>10,n_steps=>20,suffix=>"tc"})
end actions
